{:edb/db-before-t 1157 :edb/db-after-t 1158 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534470 50 #inst "2026-10-07T06:47:58.555Z" 13194139534470 true] [17592186068370 1100 "Find the general solution to the differential equation $y' = \\dfrac {x^2}{y}$." 13194139534470 false] [17592186068370 1100 "Find the general solution to the differential equation $y' = \\dfrac {x^2}{y}.$" 13194139534470 true] [17592186068370 1145 "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{\\prime } &= \\frac{x^{2}}{y} \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{x^{2}}{y} \\\\\ny\\frac{dy}{dx} &= x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int y\\frac{dy}{dx}dx &= \\int x^{2}dx \\\\\n\\int y\\frac{dy}{dx}dx &= \\int x^{2}dx \\\\\n\\int ydy &= \\int x^{2}dx \\\\\n\\frac{1}{2}y^{2} &= \\frac{1}{3}x^{3} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}y^2 = \\dfrac{1}{3}x^3 + C\n$$\n\nWe can express the solution in the form $y = y(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}y^{2} &= \\frac{1}{3}x^{3} + C \\\\\ny^{2} &= \\frac{2}{3}x^{3} + 2C \\\\\ny &= \\pm  \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}x^{3} + 2C} \\\\\ny &= \\pm  \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}x^{3} + K}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K = 2C$." 13194139534470 false] [17592186068370 1145 "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y} \\\\ y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int y\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}y^2 = \\dfrac{1}{3}x^3 + C.\n$$\n\nWe can express the solution in the form $y = y(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+C \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+2C \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+2C} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+K}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K = 2C.$" 13194139534470 true] [17592186068371 1100 "Find the general solution to the differential equation $y' + 2xy = 0$ for $y\\neq 0$." 13194139534470 false] [17592186068371 1100 "Find the general solution to the differential equation $y' + 2xy = 0$ for $y\\neq 0.$" 13194139534470 true] [17592186068371 1145 "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{\\prime } + 2xy &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx} + 2xy &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= -2xy \\\\\n\\frac{1}{y} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} &= -2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int \\frac{1}{y} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}dx &= \\int (-2x)dx \\\\\n\\int \\frac{1}{y}dy &= -2\\int xdx \\\\\n\\ln \\mid y \\mid &= -x^{2} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\ln \\vert y \\vert = -x^2 + C\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y = y(x)$. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln \\mid y \\mid &= -x^{2} + C \\\\\n\\mid y \\mid &= e^{-x^{2} + C} \\\\\n\\mid y \\mid &= e^{-x^{2}} \\cdot e^{C} \\\\\n\\mid y \\mid &= Ke^{-x^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K = e^C$ and $K > 0$.\n\nWe can drop the absolute value bars by allowing $K$ to be either positive or negative. Therefore, the most general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ke^{-x^2},\\qquad K\\ne0.\n$$" 13194139534470 false] [17592186068371 1145 "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+2xy & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2xy \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =-2\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =-{x}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\ln \\vert y \\vert = -x^2 + C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y = y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =-{x}^{2}+C \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{-{x}^{2}+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\cdot {e}^{C} \\\\ |y| & =K{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K = e^C$  and $K > 0.$ \n\nWe can drop the absolute value bars by allowing $K$ to be either positive or negative. Therefore, the most general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ke^{-x^2}.\n$$" 13194139534470 true] [17592186068372 1100 "Given that $x,y \\geq 0$, separate the variables in the following differential equation:\n\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\sqrt{2xy}$" 13194139534470 false] [17592186068372 1100 "Given that $x,y \\geq 0,$ separate the variables in the following differential equation: \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\sqrt{2xy}\n$$" 13194139534470 true] [17592186068372 1145 "A differentiable equation is *separable* if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = h(x)g(y).\n$$\n\nIf an equation is separable, we say that the variables are *separated* when it is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{g(y)} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = h(x).\n$$\n\nNotice that our differentiable equation is separable since it can be written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} =  \\sqrt{2x} \\cdot  \\sqrt{y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{y}} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} = \\sqrt{2x}\n$$" 13194139534470 true] [17592186068372 1145 "A first-order differential equation is separable when it has the form $y'=h(x)g(y)$. Here, since $x,y\\ge0$,\n$$\\sqrt{2xy}=\\sqrt{2x}\\sqrt y.$$\nWhere $y>0$, division by $\\sqrt y$ gives the separated form\n$$\\frac1{\\sqrt y}\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\sqrt{2x}.$$\nThe division does not apply at $y=0$. The original equation also admits the equilibrium solution $y=0$, which must be considered separately." 13194139534470 false] [17592186068374 1100 "Find the general solution to the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n    =\n    \\dfrac{x}{y}.$" 13194139534470 true] [17592186068374 1100 "Find the general solution to the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{x}{y}$." 13194139534470 false] [17592186068374 1145 "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{x}{y} \\\\\ny\\frac{dy}{dx} &= x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int y\\frac{dy}{dx}dx &= \\int xdx \\\\\n\\int y\\frac{dy}{dx}dx &= \\int xdx \\\\\n\\int ydy &= \\int xdx \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{2} &= \\frac{x^{2}}{2} + K \\\\\ny^{2} &= x^{2} + 2K \\\\\ny^{2} &= x^{2} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C= 2K$.\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\ny^2 = x^2 + C\n$$" 13194139534470 false] [17592186068374 1145 "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x}{y} \\\\ y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int y\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+K \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+2K \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C= 2K.$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\ny^2 = x^2 + C .\n$$" 13194139534470 true] [17592186068768 1020 #edb/ref 17592186290034 13194139534470 true] [17592186068768 1020 #edb/ref 17592186290041 13194139534470 true] [17592186068768 1020 #edb/ref 17592186290048 13194139534470 true] [17592186068768 1020 #edb/ref 17592186290055 13194139534470 true] [17592186068768 1020 #edb/ref 17592186290104 13194139534470 true] [17592186068769 1020 #edb/ref 17592186290006 13194139534470 true] [17592186068769 1020 #edb/ref 17592186290027 13194139534470 true] [17592186068769 1020 #edb/ref 17592186290111 13194139534470 true] [17592186068769 1020 #edb/ref 17592186290118 13194139534470 true] [17592186068769 1020 #edb/ref 17592186290125 13194139534470 true] [17592186068770 1020 #edb/ref 17592186290062 13194139534470 true] [17592186068770 1020 #edb/ref 17592186290069 13194139534470 true] [17592186068770 1020 #edb/ref 17592186290076 13194139534470 true] [17592186068770 1020 #edb/ref 17592186290083 13194139534470 true] [17592186068772 1020 #edb/ref 17592186290013 13194139534470 true] [17592186068772 1020 #edb/ref 17592186290020 13194139534470 true] [17592186068772 1020 #edb/ref 17592186290090 13194139534470 true] [17592186068772 1020 #edb/ref 17592186290097 13194139534470 true] [17592186069004 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534470 true] [17592186069004 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534470 false] [17592186069004 1100 "Find the general solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}\\,$ for $\\,|y|<1.$" 13194139534470 true] [17592186069004 1100 "Find the general solution to the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\sqrt{1 - y^{2}}$ for $\\mid y \\mid < 1$." 13194139534470 false] [17592186069004 1145 "The condition $|y|<1$ permits division by $\\sqrt{1-y^2}$:\n$$\\frac{dy}{\\sqrt{1-y^2}}=dx.$$\nIntegrating gives $\\arcsin y=x+C$. An explicit form is $y=\\sin(x+C)$ on intervals where $-\\pi/2<x+C<\\pi/2$, which preserves $y'=\\sqrt{1-y^2}>0$." 13194139534470 false] [17592186069004 1145 "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y & =x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y=x+C.\n$$" 13194139534470 true] [17592186069006 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534470 true] [17592186069006 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534470 false] [17592186069006 1100 "Separate the variables in the following differential equation:\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{xy^{2}}{x + 1}$" 13194139534470 false] [17592186069006 1100 "Separate the variables in the following differential equation: \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{x+1}\n$$" 13194139534470 true] [17592186069006 1145 "A differentiable equation is *separable* if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=h(x)g(y).\n$$\n\nIf an equation is separable, we say that the variables are *separated* when it is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{g(y)}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=h(x).\n$$\n\nNotice that our differentiable equation is separable since it can be written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(\\frac{x}{x+1})\\cdot {y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x}{x+1}\n$$" 13194139534470 true] [17592186069006 1145 "Factor the right-hand side as $\\frac{x}{x+1}y^2$. Where $y\\ne0$, divide both sides by $y^2$:\n$$\\frac1{y^2}\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x}{x+1}.$$\nThis is the separated form for $x\\ne-1$. The solution $y=0$ must be considered separately because division by $y^2$ excludes it." 13194139534470 false] [17592186290006 1017 #edb/ref 17592186290007 13194139534470 true] [17592186290006 1089 "q-14253" 13194139534470 true] [17592186290006 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534470 true] [17592186290006 1100 "Find the general solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}+2{y}^{2}x$ for $y\\ne 0.$" 13194139534470 true] [17592186290006 1113 #uuid "0f1d7bb9-17fc-5ea6-b46c-64a6f08a7c91" 13194139534470 true] [17592186290006 1145 "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={y}^{2}+2{y}^{2}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={y}^{2}(1+2x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1+2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (1+2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int (1+2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{y} & =x+{x}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{y}={x}^{2}+x+C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+x+C}.\n$$" 13194139534470 true] [17592186290007 1000 #edb/ref 17592186290008 13194139534470 true] [17592186290007 1000 #edb/ref 17592186290009 13194139534470 true] [17592186290007 1